\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Zero modes and the nearest-neighbor mismatch}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{On the [periodic grid](fdip-000R), let #{e} be the number
of even periods among #{N_1,\ldots,N_d}.
The centered Dirac operator has exactly #{2^e} zero momentum
blocks and
##{\dim_{\mathbb C}\ker D=2^{d+e}.}
This is the kernel dimension for the explicitly chosen
reducible module #{S}, whose dimension is #{2^d}.
The unit-weight \vocabk{graph Laplacian}{fdip-0007}
##{L_{\mathrm{nn}}=\sum_{j=1}^d
 (2I_H-T_j-T_j^{-1})}
has only the zero momentum in its scalar kernel.
Moreover #{-D^2\ne I_S\otimes L_{\mathrm{nn}}} on every
permitted grid, including grids with all periods odd.
These are finite consequences of the
[Fourier blocks](fdip-000S); the centered-difference convention
comes from \citet{Section 9.1, Equations (9.2), (9.6)-(9.7),
pp. 19-20}{wingate2025lattice}.}

\proof{
\p{The forward-difference factorization from
\ref{fdip-0014}, applied in each coordinate, gives
##{L_{\mathrm{nn}}=\sum_j(T_j-I_H)^*(T_j-I_H).}
For #{N_j\ge3}, each undirected nearest-neighbor edge occurs
once in the corresponding squared-difference energy.
On the character with momentum #{p}, the eigenvalue is
##{\begin{aligned}
\ell(p)&=\sum_j(2-2\cos p_j)\\
       &=4\sum_j\sin^2(p_j/2).
\end{aligned}}
The positive Dirac square has scalar block
#{s(p)=\sum_j\sin^2p_j}. The double-angle identity gives
##{\ell(p)-s(p)=4\sum_j\sin^4(p_j/2).}
Choose #{r_1=1} and the other labels zero. Since #{N_1\ge3},
#{\sin(\pi/N_1)>0}, so the difference is strictly positive
on that block. This proves the operator mismatch.}

\p{Every summand of #{s(p)} is nonnegative. Hence #{s(p)=0}
exactly when each #{\sin p_j=0}. In the range
#{0\le r_j<N_j}, this means #{r_j=0}, or
#{r_j=N_j/2} when #{N_j} is even. There are exactly
#{2^e} choices. On each such block #{D(p)=0} on all of #{S}.
Every other block is invertible, with two-sided inverse
##{D(p)^{-1}=-D(p)/s(p),}
because #{D(p)^2=-s(p)I_S}. Completeness of the Fourier
basis therefore gives #{\dim\ker D=2^e\dim S=2^{d+e}}.
In contrast, #{\ell(p)=0} forces #{r_j=0} for every #{j};
thus #{\dim\ker(I_S\otimes L_{\mathrm{nn}})=2^d}.}
}

\p{For #{d=1,N=4}, the alternating scalar field
#{a(x)=(-1)^x} has momentum #{p=\pi}. Its centered
difference is zero, whereas #{L_{\mathrm{nn}}a=4a}.
Consequently every field #{x\mapsto a(x)v}, #{v\in S},
is killed by #{D}. Together with the constant fields these
give the four-dimensional kernel. Write
#{K_2=(2I_H-T^2-T^{-2})/4}, so #{-D^2=I_S\otimes K_2}.
The two scalar Laplacians have the following weighted graphs:}

\tikzfigs{0.75}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  vertex/.style={circle,draw,inner sep=2pt,minimum size=6mm},
  every node/.style={inner sep=2pt}
]
\node at (0.8,1.3) {$L_{\mathrm{nn}}$};
\node[vertex] (a0) at (0,0) {$0$};
\node[vertex] (a1) at (1.6,0) {$1$};
\node[vertex] (a2) at (1.6,-1.6) {$2$};
\node[vertex] (a3) at (0,-1.6) {$3$};
\draw (a0) -- node[above=2mm] {$1$} (a1);
\draw (a1) -- node[right=2mm] {$1$} (a2);
\draw (a2) -- node[below=2mm] {$1$} (a3);
\draw (a3) -- node[left=2mm] {$1$} (a0);
\node at (4.8,1.3) {$K_2$};
\node[vertex] (b0) at (4,0) {$0$};
\node[vertex] (b2) at (5.6,0) {$2$};
\node[vertex] (b1) at (4,-1.6) {$1$};
\node[vertex] (b3) at (5.6,-1.6) {$3$};
\draw (b0) -- node[above=2mm] {$\tfrac12$} (b2);
\draw (b1) -- node[below=2mm] {$\tfrac12$} (b3);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{Left: the four unit-weight nearest-neighbor edges.
Right: the two-step edges, with vertices rearranged to display
the parity classes #{\{0,2\}} and #{\{1,3\}}.
At period four #{T^2=T^{-2}}, so
#{K_2=(I_H-T^2)/2}: the two shift contributions of weight
#{1/4} combine into one undirected edge of weight #{1/2}
for each opposite pair. The alternating field is constant on
each right-hand component, explaining its zero energy there.}

\p{For #{d=1,N=3}, momentum #{p=2\pi/3} instead gives
##{s(p)=\tfrac34,\qquad\ell(p)=3.}
Only #{r=0} is a zero momentum, yet the operators still differ.
Thus extra exact zero momenta occur precisely when at least
one period is even, whereas the square mismatch always occurs.
The excluded case #{N=2} has #{T=T^{-1}}, making its centered
difference identically zero.}

\p{The finite kernel dimension above is not a physical species
or chirality count. Friedan's Nielsen-Ninomiya theorem concerns
free fermions on the infinite lattice #{\mathbb Z^d} for odd
#{d}, with continuous torus momenta. Its Hamiltonian is
quadratic, phase invariant, translation invariant and local,
with a Hermitian momentum symbol. The locality discussion
reduces the Hamiltonian requirement to continuity; the
internal-charge projections must be smooth. The zero set is
finite and isolated, with specified relativistic
Clifford-linear behavior near each zero. Under these hypotheses
the total chiral index vanishes separately for each charge
\citet{Sections 1-2, pp. 481-484; Equations (6), (10)-(11)}{friedan1982nielsen}.
A finite sampled grid in arbitrary dimension with the chosen
reducible module computes a different quantity. The odd-period
example neither contradicts that theorem nor establishes a
continuum limit. No Lorentzian model or factorization of the
earlier weighted precision is supplied by this centered
operator.}
